SRP is the most misunderstood SOLID principle. "One responsibility" doesn't mean one method. It means **one axis of change** — one business stakeholder or one source of requirements that would cause this class to be modified.

Consider a `UserService` that handles registration, sends welcome emails, and generates user reports. These serve three different stakeholders: the product team (registration rules), the marketing team (email templates), and the analytics team (report format). When SendGrid's API changes, you're editing a class that also contains registration logic — and a subtle bug there could break user signups.

```java
public class UserService {
    public User register(UserDto dto) { /* validation + DB save */ }
    public void sendWelcomeEmail(User user) { /* SMTP logic */ }
    public String generateReport(List<User> users) { /* PDF generation */ }
}
```

Three reasons to change coexist in one class. The fix is extracting `EmailService` and `ReportService` so a change to the email provider can never accidentally break registration.

**When to violate:** A microservice with 200 total lines doesn't benefit from five single-responsibility classes. The overhead of navigating separate files exceeds the coupling cost. SRP pays off when the class genuinely serves multiple stakeholders whose requirements change independently.

The Open/Closed Principle says modules should be **open for extension but closed for modification**. The practical signal of an OCP violation is a growing if-else chain where every new feature adds another branch to the same method:

```java
public double calculateDiscount(Order order) {
    if (order.getType().equals("PREMIUM")) return 0.20;
    if (order.getType().equals("SEASONAL")) return 0.15;
    if (order.getType().equals("LOYALTY")) return 0.10;
    // Every new discount type = edit this method, retest everything
    return 0.0;
}
```

The fix is the Strategy pattern. Define a `DiscountStrategy` interface and implement each variant as a separate class. In Spring, annotate each with `@Component` and inject them as a collection — Spring discovers new strategies via component scanning, without touching the consuming code.

```java
@Component
public class PremiumDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    public boolean supports(String type) { return "PREMIUM".equals(type); }
    public double calculate(Order order) { return 0.20; }
}
// New discount = new class. Zero edits to existing code.
```

This is OCP in action: the system is extended by adding code, not modifying it.

**When to violate:** If you have exactly two cases and the product roadmap shows no third, the if-else is simpler and more readable. Extract to strategy when the third case arrives — that's when OCP starts paying for itself.

LSP says subtypes must be **substitutable** for their base types without altering correctness. The classic textbook violation is `Square extends Rectangle`, but in production Java the problem is more subtle — subtypes that throw where parents don't.

```java
public class ReadOnlyList<T> extends ArrayList<T> {
    @Override
    public boolean add(T element) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); // runtime surprise
    }
}
```

Any code accepting `List<T>` can call `add()`. `ReadOnlyList` compiles fine but throws at runtime, violating the behavioral contract. The caller has no compile-time signal that this `List` rejects mutations.

The fix is **composition over inheritance**. Wrap the list and expose only read operations — no `add()` method exists to misuse:

```java
public class ReadOnlyView<T> {
    private final List<T> delegate;
    public ReadOnlyView(List<T> source) { this.delegate = List.copyOf(source); }
    public T get(int index) { return delegate.get(index); }
    public int size() { return delegate.size(); }
    // No add() — compile-time safety, not runtime surprise
}
```

Java's own `Collections.unmodifiableList()` technically violates LSP by throwing on mutation. It's a pragmatic trade-off the JDK team chose for backward compatibility — worth recognizing as an intentional violation, not a model to follow.

ISP says clients shouldn't depend on methods they don't use. A **fat interface** forces implementors to provide stubs for methods they have no meaningful implementation for — resulting in `throw new UnsupportedOperationException()` scattered through the codebase.

```java
public interface Worker {
    void code();
    void test();
    void attendMeeting();
    void writeDocumentation();
    void deployToProduction();
}
// An intern class implements Worker — but throws on deploy and docs
```

The fix is splitting into focused interfaces that each represent one capability:

```java
public interface Coder { void code(); }
public interface Tester { void test(); }
public interface Deployer { void deployToProduction(); }
// Classes implement only the interfaces relevant to their role
```

Spring's own evolution demonstrates ISP well. `ApplicationListener<E>` — one method — replaced older event interfaces that bundled multiple event types. Spring Data's repository hierarchy (`Repository → CrudRepository → PagingAndSortingRepository → JpaRepository`) lets you depend on exactly the level of functionality you need. If you only need `save()` and `findById()`, extend `CrudRepository` and avoid pulling in JPA-specific flush and batch methods.

**When to violate:** A REST controller with 4 CRUD endpoints behind one interface is perfectly fine. Splitting `UserController` into `UserCreator`, `UserReader`, `UserUpdater`, `UserDeleter` is ISP taken past the point of usefulness.

DIP says high-level modules (business logic) should not depend on low-level modules (database drivers, HTTP clients). Both should depend on **abstractions** — interfaces defined by the high-level module and implemented by the low-level module.

Without DIP, your service is welded to its infrastructure:

```java
public class OrderService {
    // Direct dependency — can't test, can't swap databases
    private MySqlOrderRepo repo = new MySqlOrderRepo();
}
```

Switching to PostgreSQL or writing a unit test with a fake repository requires modifying `OrderService`. DIP inverts this coupling:

```java
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repo; // interface — domain defines the contract
    public OrderService(OrderRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }
}
// Test:  new OrderService(mockRepo)
// Prod:  Spring injects JpaOrderRepository automatically
```

Spring's entire IoC container is DIP materialized. Constructor injection declares what a class needs (an abstraction); the framework provides the concrete implementation. Tests inject mocks. Production injects real beans. The service never knows the difference.

DIP is the one SOLID principle I almost never violate in Spring applications. Constructor injection is so low-cost that bypassing it — using `new MySqlRepo()` directly — is a code smell with essentially no upside. The only exception is performance-critical inner loops where DI container lookup overhead matters, which is vanishingly rare.

The statement `instance = new Singleton()` looks atomic but involves three distinct operations: (1) allocate memory, (2) invoke the constructor to initialize fields, (3) assign the reference to `instance`. The JVM is free to reorder steps 2 and 3 — meaning another thread can see a non-null `instance` whose constructor hasn't finished running.

Without `volatile`, there's no happens-before edge between the constructor writes and the reference assignment. Thread B checks `instance != null`, skips the synchronized block, and returns a half-constructed object — fields are at their default values (`null`, `0`, `false`) instead of constructor-initialized values.

```java
public class ConnectionPool {
    // volatile ensures all constructor writes complete
    // before the reference becomes visible to other threads
    private static volatile ConnectionPool instance;
    private final HikariDataSource dataSource;

    private ConnectionPool() {
        this.dataSource = new HikariDataSource(config()); // heavy init
    }

    public static ConnectionPool getInstance() {
        ConnectionPool local = instance; // single volatile read
        if (local == null) {
            synchronized (ConnectionPool.class) {
                local = instance;
                if (local == null) {
                    instance = local = new ConnectionPool();
                }
            }
        }
        return local;
    }
}
```

The `local` variable optimization reduces the number of volatile reads after initialization to exactly one. Without it, the return statement performs a second volatile read. This is a minor but measurable optimization in hot paths.

The initialization-on-demand holder idiom eliminates `volatile` and `synchronized` entirely by exploiting a JVM guarantee: **a class is initialized exactly once, atomically, the first time it's accessed**. The inner `Holder` class isn't loaded until `getInstance()` is called, and the JVM's class initialization lock ensures thread safety:

```java
public class ConnectionPool {
    private ConnectionPool() { /* heavy initialization */ }

    // Holder is not loaded until getInstance() triggers it
    // JVM guarantees: one thread initializes, all others wait
    private static class Holder {
        static final ConnectionPool INSTANCE = new ConnectionPool();
    }

    public static ConnectionPool getInstance() {
        return Holder.INSTANCE; // triggers class loading on first call
    }
}
```

The enum singleton takes a fundamentally different approach. The JVM guarantees each enum constant is created exactly once during class loading, and provides built-in protection against serialization and reflection attacks:

```java
public enum ConnectionPool {
    INSTANCE;

    private final HikariDataSource dataSource;

    ConnectionPool() {
        dataSource = new HikariDataSource(buildConfig());
    }

    public Connection getConnection() throws SQLException {
        return dataSource.getConnection();
    }
}
// Usage: ConnectionPool.INSTANCE.getConnection()
```

`Constructor.newInstance()` throws `IllegalArgumentException` for enum types — reflection cannot create a second instance. During deserialization, the JVM returns the existing enum constant instead of constructing a new object. The only limitation is that enums implicitly extend `Enum` and therefore cannot extend another class.

In modern Spring applications, **`@Component` beans are singletons by default** (`@Scope("singleton")`). The container manages lifecycle, thread safety, and scope — making manual Singleton implementation unnecessary except for pre-container utilities like caches or connection pools initialized before the Spring context starts.

Factory Method relies on inheritance. A base class defines the algorithm but defers one creation step to subclasses. The framework calls the factory method, and the subclass returns the appropriate concrete type.

The key structural element is that the factory method is typically `protected abstract` — the base class knows it needs a `Notification`, but doesn't know or care whether it's email, SMS, or push:

```java
public abstract class NotificationService {
    // Template method: the algorithm is fixed
    public void sendAlert(String message) {
        Notification notification = createNotification(); // factory method
        notification.format(message);
        notification.send();
    }
    // Factory method: subclass decides what to create
    protected abstract Notification createNotification();
}

public class EmailNotificationService extends NotificationService {
    @Override
    protected Notification createNotification() {
        return new EmailNotification(smtpConfig);
    }
}

public class PushNotificationService extends NotificationService {
    @Override
    protected Notification createNotification() {
        return new PushNotification(firebaseClient);
    }
}
```

In Spring, `FactoryBean<T>` is a textbook example. You implement `FactoryBean<DataSource>` and override `getObject()` to control how the bean is created. The container calls `getObject()` — the factory method — and registers the result. The consumer injects `DataSource` without knowing the creation logic.

Abstract Factory shifts from inheritance to composition. Instead of a subclass deciding, a factory interface declares creation methods for every product in the family. Concrete factory implementations produce a complete, consistent set.

The real-world motivation is **preventing incompatible combinations**. In a payment system, a `StripeGateway` expects Stripe-specific webhook formats and refund APIs. Mixing a `StripeGateway` with a `PayPalWebhookHandler` produces runtime failures that no type system catches — unless the factory enforces consistency:

```java
public interface PaymentFactory {
    PaymentGateway createGateway();
    WebhookHandler createWebhookHandler();
    RefundProcessor createRefundProcessor();
}

public class StripeFactory implements PaymentFactory {
    @Override
    public PaymentGateway createGateway() { return new StripeGateway(apiKey); }
    @Override
    public WebhookHandler createWebhookHandler() { return new StripeWebhookHandler(secret); }
    @Override
    public RefundProcessor createRefundProcessor() { return new StripeRefundProcessor(apiKey); }
}

// Client receives a PaymentFactory — all products guaranteed compatible
@Service
public class CheckoutService {
    private final PaymentFactory factory;
    public CheckoutService(PaymentFactory factory) {
        this.factory = factory; // injected by Spring profile
    }
}
```

Adding a new payment provider (PayPal, Adyen) means implementing a new factory class — zero changes to `CheckoutService`. Adding a new product type to the family (say, `createDisputeHandler()`) requires updating the interface and all factories — this is the known trade-off.

The most critical design decision in a builder is distinguishing **required** fields from **optional** ones. Required fields belong in the builder's constructor — callers physically cannot forget them:

```java
public static Builder builder(String method, String url) {
    return new Builder(method, url); // required — can't create without these
}

public static final class Builder {
    private final String method; // required — set once in constructor
    private final String url;    // required
    private final Map<String, String> headers = new LinkedHashMap<>();
    private String body;          // optional — null by default
    private int timeoutMs = 5000; // optional — sensible default

    private Builder(String method, String url) {
        this.method = Objects.requireNonNull(method, "method required");
        this.url = Objects.requireNonNull(url, "url required");
    }

    public Builder header(String name, String value) {
        this.headers.put(name, value);
        return this; // fluent — returns Builder for chaining
    }

    public Builder body(String body)     { this.body = body; return this; }
    public Builder timeoutMs(int ms)     { this.timeoutMs = ms; return this; }
}
```

Each optional setter returns `this`, enabling the chained style: `.header(...).body(...).timeoutMs(3000).build()`. The chain reads like a specification — each method call documents what it configures. Compare this to `new HttpRequest("POST", url, null, body, 3000, 3, true, null)` where every parameter is a mystery.

Cross-field validation — constraints that involve multiple parameters — must happen in `build()`, not scattered across individual setters. A setter doesn't know what other fields have been set yet:

```java
public HttpRequest build() {
    if (("POST".equals(method) || "PUT".equals(method)) && body == null) {
        throw new IllegalStateException(method + " requests require a body");
    }
    if (timeoutMs <= 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("timeout must be positive");
    }
    return new HttpRequest(this);
}
```

The target class constructor must make **defensive copies** of mutable collections. Without this, a caller can modify the builder's map after `build()` — breaking the immutability of the supposedly-finished object:

```java
private HttpRequest(Builder b) {
    this.method    = b.method;
    this.url       = b.url;
    this.headers   = Collections.unmodifiableMap(new LinkedHashMap<>(b.headers));
    this.body      = b.body;
    this.timeoutMs = b.timeoutMs;
}
```

Builders themselves are **not thread-safe** by design. They're mutable, single-use objects meant to be created, configured, and built within a single scope. Never share a builder across threads — there's no practical reason to, and the result is a race condition that's difficult to reproduce.

In production codebases, writing builder boilerplate manually is rare. **Lombok's `@Builder`** generates the entire builder from the class fields:

```java
@Builder
@Value // makes all fields final + generates getters
public class HttpRequest {
    String method;
    String url;
    @Builder.Default int timeoutMs = 5000;
    @Singular Map<String, String> headers;
    String body;
}

// Usage:
HttpRequest req = HttpRequest.builder()
    .method("POST")
    .url("https://api.example.com")
    .header("Authorization", "Bearer token")
    .body("{\"name\": \"Alice\"}")
    .build();
```

`@Builder.Default` sets defaults for omitted fields. `@Singular` generates an `addHeader(k, v)` accumulator that builds an unmodifiable map.

For APIs where **forgetting a required field must be a compile error** (not a runtime exception), the **staged builder** pattern uses one interface per required step:

```java
public interface MethodStep { UrlStep method(String method); }
public interface UrlStep    { OptionalStep url(String url); }
public interface OptionalStep {
    OptionalStep header(String name, String value);
    HttpRequest build();
}
```

Each step returns the next interface — the caller cannot call `build()` until `method()` and `url()` have been provided. This is heavier than Lombok but guarantees at compile time that required fields are set. The pattern shows up in Spring's `WebClient` fluent API and `MockMvc` request builders.

In the classic GoF Observer, the subject maintains a list of observers and you write explicit `addObserver()`/`removeObserver()` calls. Spring eliminates this ceremony entirely. Any `@Component` bean with an `@EventListener` method is automatically registered as an observer for that event type.

The publisher injects `ApplicationEventPublisher` and fires events without knowing who listens:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repo;
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository repo, ApplicationEventPublisher events) {
        this.repo = repo;
        this.events = events;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(CreateOrderCommand cmd) {
        Order order = Order.create(cmd);
        repo.save(order);
        // OrderService has ZERO knowledge of who listens
        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlacedEvent(order.getId(), cmd.customerId()));
    }
}
```

Observers are independent beans, each handling one concern:

```java
@Component
public class EmailListener {
    @EventListener
    public void onOrderPlaced(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
        emailService.sendConfirmation(event.getOrderId());
    }
}

@Component
public class InventoryListener {
    @EventListener
    public void onOrderPlaced(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
        inventoryService.reduceStock(event.getOrderId());
    }
}
```

Adding a loyalty points listener means creating a new `@Component` class. No edits to `OrderService`. No registration code. Spring discovers it at startup through component scanning.

A subtle but critical problem with synchronous `@EventListener` is that it fires **during** the transaction. If the order save succeeds, the email sends, but then a later validation fails and the transaction rolls back — the customer receives a confirmation for an order that doesn't exist.

`@TransactionalEventListener` solves this by binding the listener to a **transaction phase**:

```java
@Component
public class InventoryListener {
    // Fires ONLY after the enclosing transaction commits successfully
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void onOrderPlaced(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
        inventoryService.reduceStock(event.getOrderId());
    }
}
```

If the transaction rolls back, the listener never fires. This prevents side effects based on data that was subsequently discarded. The `AFTER_COMMIT` phase is the most common — use `AFTER_ROLLBACK` for compensation logic (retry queues, alerts), and `BEFORE_COMMIT` when the listener's work must be part of the same transaction.

The trade-off is that `@TransactionalEventListener` silently swallows events when there's **no active transaction**. If your method isn't `@Transactional`, the event is published but never delivered. This catches teams by surprise — if your listener isn't firing, check whether the publisher method runs inside a transaction.

Synchronous event dispatch blocks the publisher until all listeners complete. For fast operations this is fine, but a listener that calls an external API or sends an email turns a 50ms order placement into a 2-second operation.

**Async listeners** move execution to a separate thread pool so the publisher returns immediately:

```java
@Component
public class AnalyticsListener {
    @Async("eventExecutor")  // runs on a dedicated thread pool
    @EventListener
    public void onOrderPlaced(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
        analyticsClient.recordPurchase(event.getCustomerId());
    }
}
```

This requires `@EnableAsync` on a configuration class and a configured `Executor` bean. With async dispatch, exceptions in listeners are swallowed unless you configure an `AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler` — log the error and push to a retry queue.

**Listener ordering** is controlled with `@Order(1)` on the method. Without it, Spring doesn't guarantee execution order. With async listeners, ordering isn't possible at all — if strict ordering matters, process events sequentially or use a message broker.

Spring events are **in-process and in-memory**. If the application crashes after publishing but before the listener completes, the event is lost. For cross-service communication, guaranteed delivery, or replay capability, you need **Kafka, RabbitMQ, or a transactional outbox pattern**. Spring events excel at within-service decoupling — decoupling `OrderService` from `EmailService` within the same JVM.

Start by defining the interface that all strategies implement. The method signature captures the common inputs and outputs — only the algorithm differs:

```java
public interface DiscountStrategy {
    String getType(); // identifies which discount this handles
    BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal originalPrice, Order order);
}
```

Each concrete strategy is a focused class with one responsibility:

```java
@Component
public class PercentageDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    public String getType() { return "PERCENTAGE"; }
    public BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal price, Order order) {
        return price.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(0.90)); // 10% off
    }
}

@Component
public class LoyaltyDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    public String getType() { return "LOYALTY"; }
    public BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal price, Order order) {
        int points = order.getCustomer().getLoyaltyPoints();
        BigDecimal bonus = BigDecimal.valueOf(points * 0.01);
        return price.subtract(bonus).max(BigDecimal.ZERO);
    }
}
```

Each strategy is annotated `@Component` so Spring discovers it automatically. The strategy's `getType()` method provides the key for lookup — no `switch` statement needed anywhere.

The most elegant strategy dispatch in Spring uses **autowired collection injection**. Spring can inject all implementations of an interface as a `List` or, with bean naming, as a `Map`. Build the lookup map in the constructor:

```java
@Service
public class DiscountService {
    private final Map<String, DiscountStrategy> strategies;

    public DiscountService(List<DiscountStrategy> strategyList) {
        // Build a lookup map keyed by each strategy's type identifier
        this.strategies = strategyList.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(DiscountStrategy::getType, Function.identity()));
    }

    public BigDecimal applyDiscount(String discountType, BigDecimal price, Order order) {
        DiscountStrategy strategy = strategies.get(discountType);
        if (strategy == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown discount: " + discountType);
        }
        return strategy.apply(price, order);
    }
}
```

Adding a new discount type — say, `SeasonalDiscount` — requires creating one `@Component` class. Zero changes to `DiscountService`. Zero changes to any existing strategy. Spring discovers the new bean at startup and it appears in the injected list automatically.

This is the Open/Closed Principle in its purest form: the system is extended by adding code, not by modifying it. The mistake to avoid is putting the if-else inside a single strategy class — that just relocates the problem without solving it.

Since `DiscountStrategy` has a single abstract method, it qualifies as a `@FunctionalInterface`. For lightweight strategies defined in configuration rather than separate files, lambdas work directly:

```java
Map<String, DiscountStrategy> strategies = Map.of(
    "PERCENTAGE", (price, order) -> price.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(0.9)),
    "FLAT",       (price, order) -> price.subtract(BigDecimal.TEN).max(BigDecimal.ZERO),
    "LOYALTY",    (price, order) -> {
        int pts = order.getCustomer().getLoyaltyPoints();
        return price.subtract(BigDecimal.valueOf(pts * 0.01)).max(BigDecimal.ZERO);
    }
);
```

This is concise and works well for strategies that are 1-3 lines of logic. The tradeoff: lambdas are harder to unit-test in isolation and don't benefit from Spring's lifecycle management (no `@PostConstruct`, no injected dependencies).

**When NOT to use Strategy at all:** if you have 2-3 simple conditions that won't grow, an if-else is simpler and more readable than a full interface + implementation hierarchy. The pattern earns its cost when the branching logic is growing, each branch has distinct complex logic, or you need to add new variants without modifying existing code. The real-world signal is: when you add a new feature and have to touch an existing if-else chain in 3 different files, it's time to refactor to Strategy.

The structural requirement is simple: the decorator and the component must share the same interface. Callers never know whether they're talking to a raw component or a decorated one — the contract is identical:

```java
public interface DataProcessor {
    String process(String data);
}

public class RawDataProcessor implements DataProcessor {
    @Override
    public String process(String data) { return data.trim(); }
}

public abstract class ProcessorDecorator implements DataProcessor {
    protected final DataProcessor wrapped;
    protected ProcessorDecorator(DataProcessor wrapped) {
        this.wrapped = wrapped;
    }
}
```

Concrete decorators override the method, add their behavior, and delegate:

```java
public class EncryptionDecorator extends ProcessorDecorator {
    public EncryptionDecorator(DataProcessor wrapped) { super(wrapped); }

    @Override
    public String process(String data) {
        String processed = wrapped.process(data); // delegate first
        return Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(processed.getBytes()); // then encrypt
    }
}

public class LoggingDecorator extends ProcessorDecorator {
    public LoggingDecorator(DataProcessor wrapped) { super(wrapped); }

    @Override
    public String process(String data) {
        log.info("Input: {}", data);
        String result = wrapped.process(data);
        log.info("Output: {}", result);
        return result;
    }
}
```

Composition happens at the call site — stack decorators in any order you need:

```java
DataProcessor pipeline = new LoggingDecorator(
    new EncryptionDecorator(
        new RawDataProcessor()));
pipeline.process("  hello world  "); // logs → encrypts → trims
```

The outermost decorator (`LoggingDecorator`) processes first. Each layer is independently removable and testable.

When a Spring bean implements at least one interface, Spring can create a JDK dynamic proxy. The JVM's `java.lang.reflect.Proxy` class generates a new class at runtime that implements the same interfaces as the target bean. This generated proxy receives all method calls, applies the advice (transaction logic, caching logic), and delegates to the actual bean.

Conceptually, what Spring generates looks like this:

```java
// Spring generates something equivalent at runtime
public class OrderServiceProxy implements OrderServiceInterface {
    private final OrderServiceInterface target; // the real bean
    private final TransactionManager txManager;

    @Override
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        TransactionStatus tx = txManager.begin();
        try {
            target.placeOrder(order); // delegate to real bean
            txManager.commit(tx);
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            txManager.rollback(tx);
            throw e;
        }
    }
}
```

The critical constraint: JDK dynamic proxies only work with **interfaces**. The proxy implements `OrderServiceInterface`, not `OrderServiceImpl`. If you inject by concrete class type (`@Autowired OrderServiceImpl impl`), Spring throws a `BeanNotOfRequiredType` exception because the proxy is not an instance of the implementation class. Always inject by interface when using JDK proxies.

When a bean has no interface, Spring falls back to **CGLIB** (Code Generation Library). CGLIB generates a **subclass** of your bean at runtime, overriding methods to add the advice. Since Spring Boot 2.0, CGLIB is actually the **default** proxy mechanism (`spring.aop.proxy-target-class=true`), even when interfaces are present.

```java
// CGLIB generates a subclass at runtime
public class OrderService$$EnhancerByCGLIB extends OrderService {
    @Override
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        // Start transaction
        super.placeOrder(order); // delegate to parent (your code)
        // Commit transaction
    }
}
```

CGLIB proxies allow injecting by concrete class type — no interface required. But they have their own constraint: **`final` classes and `final` methods cannot be proxied**. CGLIB works by subclassing, and Java prevents extending `final` classes or overriding `final` methods. If you mark your service class `final`, Spring silently skips proxying — your `@Transactional` annotation has no effect, with no error at startup.

You can verify which proxy type is active at runtime using `AopUtils.isJdkDynamicProxy(bean)` or `AopUtils.isCglibProxy(bean)`. This is useful when debugging proxy-related issues in environments where Spring configuration differs between test and production.

This is the single most common Spring AOP bug, and understanding why it happens requires understanding the proxy architecture. When `methodA()` calls `this.methodB()`, the call goes directly to the target object — it **bypasses the proxy entirely**:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        orderRepo.save(order);
    }

    public void processMultiple(List<Order> orders) {
        for (Order o : orders) {
            this.placeOrder(o); // calls the REAL method, NOT the proxy
            // @Transactional has NO effect here!
        }
    }
}
```

When `processMultiple` calls `this.placeOrder()`, `this` refers to the actual `OrderService` instance, not the proxy wrapper. The proxy only intercepts calls that come **from outside the bean** — through the injected proxy reference.

Three fixes exist, each with trade-offs. The cleanest is **extracting the method into a separate bean** so the call crosses bean boundaries and goes through the proxy. The second is **self-injection** — inject the bean into itself to force routing through the proxy:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired private OrderService self; // proxy-aware self-reference

    public void processMultiple(List<Order> orders) {
        for (Order o : orders) {
            self.placeOrder(o); // goes through the proxy → transaction starts
        }
    }
}
```

The third option is **AspectJ weaving** — compile-time or load-time bytecode modification that injects the advice directly into your class, eliminating the proxy indirection entirely. AspectJ can intercept self-invocation, private methods, and `final` methods — everything proxies cannot. But it adds build complexity and is rarely needed in typical Spring applications.

In a rigorous DDD application, the **domain module defines the Repository interface** using only domain types. No Spring annotations, no JPA imports, no `Pageable` — just the domain's language:

```java
// Domain layer — zero framework imports
public interface OrderRepository {
    Order findById(OrderId id);
    List<Order> findActiveOrdersForCustomer(CustomerId customerId);
    void save(Order order);
    void remove(Order order);
}
```

The **infrastructure module provides the JPA implementation**, translating between domain objects and JPA entities:

```java
// Infrastructure layer — knows about JPA, Spring Data
@Repository
public class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    private final SpringDataOrderRepo springRepo; // extends JpaRepository

    @Override
    public Order findById(OrderId id) {
        OrderEntity entity = springRepo.findById(id.value())
            .orElseThrow(() -> new OrderNotFoundException(id));
        return OrderMapper.toDomain(entity); // JPA entity → domain object
    }

    @Override
    public void save(Order order) {
        springRepo.save(OrderMapper.toEntity(order));
    }
}
```

Contrast this with the common Spring Data approach, which is functionally a DAO:

```java
// This is a DAO in DDD terms — it exposes JPA concepts
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    List<Order> findByCustomerIdAndStatus(Long customerId, OrderStatus status);
    // Pageable, flush(), saveAndFlush() — persistence concepts leak through
}
```

The first approach keeps the domain layer persistence-ignorant. If you switch from PostgreSQL to MongoDB, you write a new infrastructure implementation — the domain module never changes. The second approach is simpler but tightly couples your domain to Spring Data JPA.

The traditional GoF Template Method uses inheritance. A base class defines the algorithm as a `final` method (preventing subclasses from changing the structure) and declares `abstract` methods for the variable steps:

```java
public abstract class ReportGenerator {
    // Template method — algorithm skeleton, final to prevent override
    public final void generate(String title) {
        openDocument(title);
        writeHeader(title);
        writeBody();          // abstract — subclass provides this
        writeFooter();
        closeDocument();
    }

    protected abstract void writeBody(); // the only step that varies

    private void openDocument(String title) { /* invariant */ }
    private void writeHeader(String title)  { /* invariant */ }
    private void writeFooter()              { /* invariant */ }
    private void closeDocument()            { /* invariant */ }
}

public class SalesReportGenerator extends ReportGenerator {
    @Override
    protected void writeBody() {
        // Only this part changes per report type
        fetchSalesData().forEach(row -> render(row));
    }
}
```

The base class controls the flow. Subclasses fill in the gaps. This is inversion of control at the class level — the framework calls you, not the other way around.

The pattern's weakness emerges when overused: making every step `abstract` creates rigid hierarchies. If `writeHeader()` and `writeFooter()` also become abstract, every subclass must implement 5 methods even when it only cares about one. This leads to the exact boilerplate the pattern was meant to eliminate.

Spring modernizes Template Method by replacing inheritance with **functional callbacks**. Instead of extending `JdbcTemplate`, you pass your variable logic as lambda arguments. The template handles the invariant boilerplate:

```java
@Repository
public class UserRepository {
    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;

    public UserRepository(JdbcTemplate jdbc) { this.jdbc = jdbc; }

    public List<User> findByDepartment(String dept) {
        // JdbcTemplate handles: connection, statement, execution,
        // exception translation, resource cleanup
        // YOU provide: the SQL and the RowMapper
        return jdbc.query(
            "SELECT id, name, email FROM users WHERE department = ?",
            (rs, rowNum) -> new User(       // RowMapper — the variable step
                rs.getLong("id"),
                rs.getString("name"),
                rs.getString("email")
            ),
            dept
        );
    }
}
```

The invariant steps `JdbcTemplate` handles internally are substantial: acquiring a `Connection` from the `DataSource`, creating a `PreparedStatement`, binding parameters, executing the query, iterating the `ResultSet` and calling your `RowMapper` for each row, translating vendor-specific `SQLExceptions` into Spring's `DataAccessException` hierarchy, and closing the `Connection` in a `finally` block regardless of success or failure.

The callback interfaces — `RowMapper<T>`, `ResultSetExtractor<T>`, `PreparedStatementSetter` — are all functional interfaces, so lambdas work directly. This eliminates the need for a class hierarchy entirely. One `JdbcTemplate` instance handles every query in your application.

Template Method isn't unique to `JdbcTemplate` — it's a recurring structural pattern across Spring's entire framework:

**RestTemplate** handles the HTTP request lifecycle. It manages connection pooling, serialization/deserialization, error handling, and retry logic. You provide the URI, HTTP method, and response type:

```java
// RestTemplate owns: connection, serialization, error handling
// You provide: URL, expected type
User user = restTemplate.getForObject("/api/users/{id}", User.class, 42);
```

**TransactionTemplate** manages transaction boundaries programmatically. It handles begin/commit/rollback and exception translation. You provide the business logic as a callback:

```java
transactionTemplate.execute(status -> {
    orderRepo.save(order);
    paymentService.charge(order.getTotal());
    return null; // TransactionTemplate commits if no exception
});
```

This is useful when `@Transactional` annotation-based AOP doesn't fit — for example, when you need different propagation behavior within a single method, or when you're working outside of Spring-managed beans.

**The common thread** across all Spring templates: the framework owns the boilerplate (connection management, error handling, resource cleanup), you own the business intent (SQL, HTTP call, transaction body). The callback-based variant is more flexible than classical Template Method because it doesn't require inheritance — you compose behavior through function references, not class hierarchies.

The `@CircuitBreaker` annotation wraps the method and invokes a fallback when the circuit is open or the call throws a recorded exception:

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final PaymentGatewayClient gateway;

    public PaymentService(PaymentGatewayClient gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "paymentService", fallbackMethod = "paymentFallback")
    public PaymentResult processPayment(PaymentRequest request) {
        return gateway.charge(request); // remote call to payment provider
    }

    // Fallback — same return type, same params + Throwable
    private PaymentResult paymentFallback(PaymentRequest request, Throwable ex) {
        log.warn("Payment circuit open, deferring order {}: {}",
            request.getOrderId(), ex.getMessage());
        return PaymentResult.deferred(request.getOrderId());
    }
}
```

A good fallback provides **graceful degradation**, not just a different exception. Options include: returning cached data, queuing the request for later processing, returning a default/partial response, or routing to an alternative provider. A fallback that throws `PaymentUnavailableException` is only marginally better than no fallback — the caller still can't complete the operation.

The fallback method must have the **same return type** and the **same parameters** as the original method, plus a `Throwable` parameter at the end. If the signatures don't match, Resilience4j silently ignores the fallback — a common debugging headache.

Resilience4j decorators stack. The typical production pattern is **Retry → Circuit Breaker → Rate Limiter**, applied in that order. Retry handles transient failures (network blip), and only if retries are exhausted does the failure count toward the circuit breaker threshold:

```java
@Retry(name = "paymentService", fallbackMethod = "paymentFallback")
@CircuitBreaker(name = "paymentService", fallbackMethod = "paymentFallback")
public PaymentResult processPayment(PaymentRequest request) {
    return gateway.charge(request);
}
```

Configure the retry to fire before the circuit breaker evaluates the call. This prevents a single transient timeout from counting as a circuit breaker failure.

Exposing circuit breaker state in **Spring Boot Actuator** gives your operations team visibility:

```yaml
management:
  health:
    circuitbreakers:
      enabled: true   # adds circuit breaker state to /actuator/health
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health
```

For programmatic monitoring, inject `CircuitBreakerRegistry` and query state directly:

```java
@Component
public class CircuitBreakerMonitor {
    private final CircuitBreakerRegistry registry;

    public CircuitBreakerMonitor(CircuitBreakerRegistry registry) {
        this.registry = registry;
    }

    public String getPaymentCircuitState() {
        return registry.circuitBreaker("paymentService")
            .getState().toString(); // CLOSED, OPEN, or HALF_OPEN
    }
}
```

In production, alert when any circuit breaker enters OPEN state. A breaker that stays open for more than a few minutes indicates a sustained downstream failure that needs human investigation — not just automatic recovery.

Start by making your repository extend `JpaSpecificationExecutor<T>`, then define specification factory methods in a dedicated class:

```java
public interface ProductRepository
        extends JpaRepository<Product, Long>,
                JpaSpecificationExecutor<Product> {}
```

Each specification is a static method returning a lambda. The critical design decision is **null-safety** — return `cb.conjunction()` (an always-true predicate) when the filter is absent, so specifications compose cleanly without null checks:

```java
public class ProductSpecs {

    public static Specification<Product> hasCategory(String category) {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            category == null ? cb.conjunction()
                : cb.equal(root.get("category"), category);
    }

    public static Specification<Product> priceBetween(BigDecimal min, BigDecimal max) {
        return (root, query, cb) -> {
            if (min == null && max == null) return cb.conjunction();
            if (min == null) return cb.lessThanOrEqualTo(root.get("price"), max);
            if (max == null) return cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("price"), min);
            return cb.between(root.get("price"), min, max);
        };
    }

    public static Specification<Product> nameLike(String keyword) {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            keyword == null ? cb.conjunction()
                : cb.like(cb.lower(root.get("name")),
                    "%" + keyword.toLowerCase() + "%");
    }

    public static Specification<Product> inStock() {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            cb.greaterThan(root.get("stockQuantity"), 0);
    }
}
```

Each specification is independently testable, reusable across different query methods, and handles its own null case gracefully.

The real power emerges in composition. The service builds a query by combining only the active filters:

```java
@Service
public class ProductSearchService {
    private final ProductRepository repo;

    public ProductSearchService(ProductRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }

    public Page<Product> search(ProductFilter filter, Pageable pageable) {
        Specification<Product> spec = Specification
            .where(ProductSpecs.hasCategory(filter.getCategory()))
            .and(ProductSpecs.priceBetween(filter.getMinPrice(), filter.getMaxPrice()))
            .and(ProductSpecs.nameLike(filter.getKeyword()));

        if (filter.isInStockOnly()) {
            spec = spec.and(ProductSpecs.inStock());
        }

        return repo.findAll(spec, pageable);
    }
}
```

`Specification.where(a).and(b).or(c)` reads fluently and each combinator returns a new `Specification` without modifying the original. Because absent filters return `cb.conjunction()` (always true), the AND composition is always safe — null filters are no-ops.

The composed specification translates to a **single SQL query** with appropriate WHERE conditions joined by AND/OR. The database optimizer handles index usage and execution planning. For specifications that join related tables (filtering products by tag name via `root.join("tags")`), be aware that multiple joins on the same table can produce duplicate rows — add `query.distinct(true)` in the specification to eliminate them.

Each specification can be **tested in isolation** with a `@DataJpaTest`. Seed the database with known data, apply a single specification, and assert the results:

```java
@DataJpaTest
class ProductSpecsTest {
    @Autowired ProductRepository repo;

    @Test
    void priceBetween_filtersCorrectly() {
        repo.save(new Product("A", new BigDecimal("10")));
        repo.save(new Product("B", new BigDecimal("50")));
        repo.save(new Product("C", new BigDecimal("100")));

        List<Product> results = repo.findAll(
            ProductSpecs.priceBetween(new BigDecimal("20"), new BigDecimal("80")));

        assertThat(results).extracting("name").containsExactly("B");
    }
}
```

This is much cleaner than testing a monolithic query method with many parameters — each specification is a focused unit.

**When NOT to use Specifications:** for fixed, static queries, a `@Query` annotation with JPQL is simpler and more readable. Specifications add value when filters are **dynamic** — user-driven search pages, admin dashboards with configurable filters, API endpoints with optional query parameters. If every query in your application has a fixed set of conditions known at compile time, the Criteria API indirection is unnecessary complexity.

**Querydsl** is an alternative that generates type-safe Q-classes from your entities. Instead of string-based field names like `root.get("category")`, you write `QProduct.product.category.eq(category)` — compile-time field checking eliminates typos. The tradeoff is build complexity (annotation processing, generated classes).

The command side is responsible for **mutating state**. Commands are explicit intent objects — `CreateOrderCommand`, `ConfirmOrderCommand`, `CancelOrderCommand` — each carrying the data needed for one operation. A command handler validates the request, loads or creates the domain aggregate, applies business rules, and persists the result.

```java
public record CreateOrderCommand(String customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {}

@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;
    private String customerId;
    private OrderStatus status;
    @OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private List<OrderLineItem> items;

    public static Order create(CreateOrderCommand cmd) {
        Order order = new Order();
        order.customerId = cmd.customerId();
        order.status = OrderStatus.PENDING;
        order.items = new ArrayList<>(cmd.items());
        order.validateMinimumOrderValue(); // business rule
        return order;
    }

    public void confirm() {
        if (status != OrderStatus.PENDING)
            throw new IllegalStateException("Only PENDING orders can be confirmed");
        this.status = OrderStatus.CONFIRMED;
    }
}
```

The command service orchestrates the operation and publishes events for the read side:

```java
@Service
@Transactional
public class OrderCommandService {
    private final OrderRepository writeRepo;
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;

    public Long createOrder(CreateOrderCommand cmd) {
        Order order = Order.create(cmd);
        writeRepo.save(order);
        events.publishEvent(new OrderCreatedEvent(order.getId(), cmd.customerId()));
        return order.getId();
    }
}
```

The domain model here is deliberately rich — it contains validation, state transitions, and business rules. This is the appropriate place for complexity.

The query side returns **read-optimized views** that contain exactly the data a specific consumer needs — no more, no less. Spring Data interface projections are ideal for this:

```java
// Flat projection — no domain logic, just data for the UI
public interface OrderSummary {
    Long getId();
    String getCustomerId();
    String getStatus();
    BigDecimal getTotalAmount();
    LocalDateTime getCreatedAt();
}

// Read-optimized repository
public interface OrderQueryRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    // Spring generates: SELECT id, customer_id, status, total, created_at FROM orders
    // No JOINs, no eager loading, no entity construction
    List<OrderSummary> findByCustomerId(String customerId);

    @Query("SELECT o.id as id, o.status as status, COUNT(i) as itemCount " +
           "FROM Order o LEFT JOIN o.items i GROUP BY o.id, o.status")
    List<OrderDashboardView> findDashboardSummaries();
}
```

The query service uses `@Transactional(readOnly = true)`, which tells Hibernate to **skip dirty checking**. In a normal transaction, Hibernate tracks every loaded entity for changes — comparing field values at flush time to detect modifications. With `readOnly = true`, this tracking is disabled entirely, giving a measurable performance improvement for read-heavy workloads:

```java
@Service
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public class OrderQueryService {
    private final OrderQueryRepository queryRepo;

    public List<OrderSummary> getOrdersByCustomer(String customerId) {
        return queryRepo.findByCustomerId(customerId);
    }
}
```

The read model is deliberately dumb — flat DTOs with no business logic, no state transitions, no validation. Its only job is to serve data to consumers as efficiently as possible.

In the lightweight form (same database, separate service classes), the read and write models share a database and are always consistent. The query repository reads the same tables the command service writes to.

The advanced form uses **separate datastores** — the command side writes to a normalized relational database, and events propagate changes to a read-optimized store (Elasticsearch for full-text search, Redis for dashboards, a denormalized read DB for fast queries). This enables **independent scaling** — add read replicas without affecting write throughput.

The bridge between write and read is **events**. The command service publishes domain events (`OrderCreatedEvent`, `OrderConfirmedEvent`). Event handlers on the read side update the read model:

```java
@Component
public class OrderProjectionUpdater {
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void onOrderCreated(OrderCreatedEvent event) {
        // Update the read-optimized view store
        readStore.save(new OrderSummaryProjection(
            event.getOrderId(), event.getCustomerId(), "PENDING"));
    }
}
```

The trade-off is **eventual consistency**. The read model may lag behind the write model — a user creates an order and immediately queries the dashboard, but the projection hasn't been updated yet. The application must be designed to handle this: show a "processing" state, use optimistic UI updates, or accept a brief delay.

**CQRS is not Event Sourcing.** They're frequently combined but are fully independent patterns. Event Sourcing stores every state change as an immutable event (the event log IS the source of truth). CQRS separates read and write models. You can have CQRS with a traditional database and no event log at all.
